'''argot''' (är′gō or är′gọt), ''n.'' [F.; origin obscure.] The conventional slang of a class, originally that of thieves and vagabonds, devised for purposes of disguise and concealment; cant; slang.
{{smaller block|''Argot'' is formed . . . by the adoption of foreign words, by the absolute suppression of grammar, by grotesque tropes, wild catachresis, and allegorical metonymy.
{{right|''Farrar.''}}}}
{{smaller block|Words or expressions in an ancient language, if they happen to coincide with some modern ''argot'' or vulgarism, take on a grotesque association which is not due at all to the phrase itself, but which makes the phrase seem much bolder than it really is. {{right|''Quarterly Rev.'', CLXII. 177.}}}}

